{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-76493" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\frac{7}{2}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to the nearest integer?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989949/state.json" "file_sha256" "eabf4ce4bd136732a917bc2e464d97ad96bd65ea01b91fd4bfd058bb8cc5e72d"} {"question" "q-76493" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{2}{7}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{2}{7})=106.601\\,549{…}^{∘}≈{106.602}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${106.602}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={106.602}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={73.398}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={73.398}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989949/assets/e378560486eca17f870b21743718c9f984f2bd3bf5609601f1cea674e15018e6.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{73.398}^{∘} \\\\  & ={253.398}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=106.602\\cdot 253.398≈27\\,013,\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989949/state.json" "file_sha256" "eabf4ce4bd136732a917bc2e464d97ad96bd65ea01b91fd4bfd058bb8cc5e72d"} {"question" "q-76493" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989949/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8407dfa0a6c69ded39f8ce45996613a479be2c130d295e5a176ba2da09636cee"} {"question" "q-76493" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989949/state.json" "file_sha256" "eabf4ce4bd136732a917bc2e464d97ad96bd65ea01b91fd4bfd058bb8cc5e72d"} {"question" "q-76493" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12\\,156"] ["math" "15\\,681"] ["math" "21\\,642"] ["math" "27\\,013"] ["math" "31\\,805"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989949/state.json" "file_sha256" "eabf4ce4bd136732a917bc2e464d97ad96bd65ea01b91fd4bfd058bb8cc5e72d"} {"question" "q-76493" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "27\\,013"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989949/state.json" "file_sha256" "eabf4ce4bd136732a917bc2e464d97ad96bd65ea01b91fd4bfd058bb8cc5e72d"} {"question" "q-120478" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following equations has no solutions for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}?$\n\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\frac{3}{2}$\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{5}{8}$\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{3}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989949/state.json" "file_sha256" "eabf4ce4bd136732a917bc2e464d97ad96bd65ea01b91fd4bfd058bb8cc5e72d"} {"question" "q-120478" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that the range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equations II and III have no solutions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989949/state.json" "file_sha256" "eabf4ce4bd136732a917bc2e464d97ad96bd65ea01b91fd4bfd058bb8cc5e72d"} {"question" "q-120478" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989949/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8407dfa0a6c69ded39f8ce45996613a479be2c130d295e5a176ba2da09636cee"} {"question" "q-120478" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989949/state.json" "file_sha256" "eabf4ce4bd136732a917bc2e464d97ad96bd65ea01b91fd4bfd058bb8cc5e72d"} {"question" "q-120478" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989949/state.json" "file_sha256" "eabf4ce4bd136732a917bc2e464d97ad96bd65ea01b91fd4bfd058bb8cc5e72d"} {"question" "q-120478" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989949/state.json" "file_sha256" "eabf4ce4bd136732a917bc2e464d97ad96bd65ea01b91fd4bfd058bb8cc5e72d"} {"question" "q-120485" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following equations has no solutions for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}?$\n\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=7$ $$\n- $\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+2=2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ $$\n- $7\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-5$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989949/state.json" "file_sha256" "eabf4ce4bd136732a917bc2e464d97ad96bd65ea01b91fd4bfd058bb8cc5e72d"} {"question" "q-120485" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We first simplify the given equations, and get\n\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=7$ $$\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\frac{5}{7}$\n\nNow, recall that the range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation III has no solutions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989949/state.json" "file_sha256" "eabf4ce4bd136732a917bc2e464d97ad96bd65ea01b91fd4bfd058bb8cc5e72d"} {"question" "q-120485" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989949/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8407dfa0a6c69ded39f8ce45996613a479be2c130d295e5a176ba2da09636cee"} {"question" "q-120485" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989949/state.json" "file_sha256" "eabf4ce4bd136732a917bc2e464d97ad96bd65ea01b91fd4bfd058bb8cc5e72d"} {"question" "q-120485" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989949/state.json" "file_sha256" "eabf4ce4bd136732a917bc2e464d97ad96bd65ea01b91fd4bfd058bb8cc5e72d"} {"question" "q-120485" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989949/state.json" "file_sha256" "eabf4ce4bd136732a917bc2e464d97ad96bd65ea01b91fd4bfd058bb8cc5e72d"} {"question" "q-274858" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find, **in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place**, the two solutions to the equation $2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-3\\sqrt{2}=\\sqrt{2}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989949/state.json" "file_sha256" "eabf4ce4bd136732a917bc2e464d97ad96bd65ea01b91fd4bfd058bb8cc5e72d"} {"question" "q-274858" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-3\\sqrt{2} & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =\\sqrt{2}+3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =4\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nThen, we find the principal value of $x,$ rounded to three decimal places:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}})≈1.209\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}≈1.209.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the $4$th quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}≈1.209.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989949/assets/2c58ae6d53b409e4fac7c4fb5c4d96b08585b1c2ee8a4f1048f4461026d748af.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ≈2\\pi -1.209 \\\\  & ≈5.074\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are $x=1.2$ and $x=5.1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989949/state.json" "file_sha256" "eabf4ce4bd136732a917bc2e464d97ad96bd65ea01b91fd4bfd058bb8cc5e72d"} {"question" "q-274858" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989949/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8407dfa0a6c69ded39f8ce45996613a479be2c130d295e5a176ba2da09636cee"} {"question" "q-274858" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989949/state.json" "file_sha256" "eabf4ce4bd136732a917bc2e464d97ad96bd65ea01b91fd4bfd058bb8cc5e72d"} {"question" "q-274858" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1.2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989949/state.json" "file_sha256" "eabf4ce4bd136732a917bc2e464d97ad96bd65ea01b91fd4bfd058bb8cc5e72d"} {"question" "q-274858" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989949/state.json" "file_sha256" "eabf4ce4bd136732a917bc2e464d97ad96bd65ea01b91fd4bfd058bb8cc5e72d"} {"question" "q-274858" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5.1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989949/state.json" "file_sha256" "eabf4ce4bd136732a917bc2e464d97ad96bd65ea01b91fd4bfd058bb8cc5e72d"} {"question" "q-325107" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the **exact** solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ for $-2\\pi <x<0.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989949/state.json" "file_sha256" "eabf4ce4bd136732a917bc2e464d97ad96bd65ea01b91fd4bfd058bb8cc5e72d"} {"question" "q-325107" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=arccos\\,(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is not in the domain $-2\\pi <x<0.$ To get a solution within the required domain, we subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}-2\\pi =-\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989949/assets/17803dc8d1d3ca2e31586969797b401d5d0a2d958bd6628387b512974076df7a.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the fourth quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $X=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ is not in the domain $-2\\pi <x<0.$ To get a solution within the required domain, we subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}-2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ and $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989949/state.json" "file_sha256" "eabf4ce4bd136732a917bc2e464d97ad96bd65ea01b91fd4bfd058bb8cc5e72d"} {"question" "q-325107" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989949/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8407dfa0a6c69ded39f8ce45996613a479be2c130d295e5a176ba2da09636cee"} {"question" "q-325107" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989949/state.json" "file_sha256" "eabf4ce4bd136732a917bc2e464d97ad96bd65ea01b91fd4bfd058bb8cc5e72d"} {"question" "q-325107" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{11\\pi}{6}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989949/state.json" "file_sha256" "eabf4ce4bd136732a917bc2e464d97ad96bd65ea01b91fd4bfd058bb8cc5e72d"} {"question" "q-325107" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989949/state.json" "file_sha256" "eabf4ce4bd136732a917bc2e464d97ad96bd65ea01b91fd4bfd058bb8cc5e72d"} {"question" "q-325107" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{\\pi}{6}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989949/state.json" "file_sha256" "eabf4ce4bd136732a917bc2e464d97ad96bd65ea01b91fd4bfd058bb8cc5e72d"}] "usage" {} "basis" 1002 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
